{"tool_name":"shorah","contents":["biocontainers","galaxy","bioschemas","biotools","oeb"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"shorah","identifiers":["biotools:shorah","doi:10.1186/1471-2105-12-119","doi:10.1093/nar/gkq655","doi:10.1089/cmb.2009.0164","doi:10.1186/1471-2164-14-501"],"license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"ShoRAH (Short Reads Assembly into Haplotypes)","home_url":"https://github.com/cbg-ethz/shorah","keywords":["Bayes Theorem","Cluster Analysis","Data Interpretation, Statistical","Genome, Viral","HIV-1","Haplotypes","Hepacivirus","High-Throughput Nucleotide Sequencing","Humans","Likelihood Functions","Models, Genetic","Polymorphism, Single Nucleotide","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Metagenomics","Sequencing","Genetics"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":3365},"biotools":{"id":"shorah","home":"https://github.com/cbg-ethz/shorah","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Inference of a population from a set of short reads. The package contains programs that support mapping of reads to a reference genome, correcting sequencing errors by locally clustering reads in small windows of the alignment, reconstructing a minimal set of global haplotypes that explain the reads, and estimating the frequencies of the inferred haplotypes.","addition_date":"2017-01-13T13:16:27Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-25T13:58:24.374484Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"version":["1.1.3","1.99.0"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:shorah","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"osvaldo.zagordi@bsse.ethz.ch","name":"Osvaldo Zagordi","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-9082-8607","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"email":"david.seifert@bsse.ethz.ch","name":"David Seifert","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4739-5110","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"],"url":"https://github.com/SoapZA"},{"email":"ivan.topolsky@sib.swiss","name":"Ivan Topolsky","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7561-0810","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"],"url":"https://www.sib.swiss/directory?cn=ivan%20topolsky"},{"email":"susana.posada@bsse.ethz.ch","name":"Susana Posada C\u00e9spedes","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7459-8186","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"],"url":"https://github.com/sposadac"},{"email":"kerensa@unsw.edu.au","name":"Kerensa McElroy","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer"]},{"gridid":"grid.419765.8","name":"SIB Swiss Institute of Bioinformatics","rorid":"002n09z45","typeEntity":"Institute","url":"https://www.sib.swiss/"},{"gridid":"grid.5801.c","name":"ETHZ","rorid":"05a28rw58","typeEntity":"Institute","url":"https://www.ethz.ch/"}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"https://github.com/cbg-ethz/shorah/blob/master/README.md"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["DrYak"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"cmd":"`shorah shotgun --bam REF_aln.bam --fasta cohort_consensus.fasta -x 100000 -w 102 -R 42`","input":[{"data":{"term":"DNA sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_3494"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]},{"data":{"term":"Nucleic acid sequence alignment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1383"},"format":[{"term":"BAM","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2572"}]}],"note":"Takes reads in BAM format aligned against a FASTA consensus.\n\n - *W*indow size selected with `-w`\n - Ma*x*imum coverage selected with `-x`\n - *R*andom seed for reproducibility selected with `-R`\n\nProduces a series of local haplotype (in  `/support` subdirectory, one FAST file per window), and SNVs (in VCF format).","operation":[{"term":"Haplotype mapping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0487"},{"term":"Variant calling","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3227"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Genotype/phenotype report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0920"},"format":[{"term":"CSV","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3752"},{"term":"VCF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3016"}]},{"data":{"term":"Haplotype map","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1863"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}]}],"maturity":"Mature","name":"ShoRAH","operating_system":["Linux","Mac"],"owner":"SIB","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkq655","metadata":{"abstract":"Next-generation sequencing technologies can be used to analyse genetically heterogeneous samples at unprecedented detail. The high coverage achievable with these methods enables the detection of many low-frequency variants. However, sequencing errors complicate the analysis of mixed populations and result in inflated estimates of genetic diversity. We developed a probabilistic Bayesian approach to minimize the effect of errors on the detection of minority variants. We applied it to pyrosequencing data obtained from a 1.5kb-fragment of the HIV-1 gag/pol gene in two control and two clinical samples. The effect of PCR amplification was analysed. Error correction resulted in a two- and five-fold decrease of the pyrosequencing base substitution rate, from 0.05 to 0.03 and from 0.25 to 0.05 in the non-PCR and PCR-amplified samples, respectively. We were able to detect viral clones as rare as 0.1 with perfect sequence reconstruction. Probabilistic haplotype inference outperforms the counting-based calling method in both precision and recall. Genetic diversity observed within and between two clinical samples resulted in various patterns of phenotypic drug resistance and suggests a close epidemiological link. We conclude that pyrosequencing can be used to investigate genetically diverse samples with high accuracy if technical errors are properly treated. \u00a9 2010 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Beerenwinkel N."},{"name":"Daumer M."},{"name":"Klein R."},{"name":"Zagordi O."}],"citationCount":169,"date":"2010-11-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nucleic Acids Research","title":"Error correction of next-generation sequencing data and reliable estimation of HIV quasispecies"},"pmcid":"PMC2995073","pmid":"20671025","type":["Method"]},{"doi":"10.1186/1471-2105-12-119","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: With next-generation sequencing technologies, experiments that were considered prohibitive only a few years ago are now possible. However, while these technologies have the ability to produce enormous volumes of data, the sequence reads are prone to error. This poses fundamental hurdles when genetic diversity is investigated.Results: We developed ShoRAH, a computational method for quantifying genetic diversity in a mixed sample and for identifying the individual clones in the population, while accounting for sequencing errors. The software was run on simulated data and on real data obtained in wet lab experiments to assess its reliability.Conclusions: ShoRAH is implemented in C++, Python, and Perl and has been tested under Linux and Mac OS X. Source code is available under the GNU General Public License at http://www.cbg.ethz.ch/software/shorah. \u00a9 2011 Zagordi et al; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.","authors":[{"name":"Beerenwinkel N."},{"name":"Bhattacharya A."},{"name":"Eriksson N."},{"name":"Zagordi O."}],"citationCount":207,"date":"2011-04-26T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Bioinformatics","title":"ShoRAH: Estimating the genetic diversity of a mixed sample from next-generation sequencing data"},"pmcid":"PMC3113935","pmid":"21521499","type":["Method"]},{"doi":"10.1186/1471-2164-14-501","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: Deep sequencing is a powerful tool for assessing viral genetic diversity. Such experiments harness the high coverage afforded by next generation sequencing protocols by treating sequencing reads as a population sample. Distinguishing true single nucleotide variants (SNVs) from sequencing errors remains challenging, however. Current protocols are characterised by high false positive rates, with results requiring time consuming manual checking.Results: By statistical modelling, we show that if multiple variant sites are considered at once, SNVs can be called reliably from high coverage viral deep sequencing data at frequencies lower than the error rate of the sequencing technology, and that SNV calling accuracy increases as true sequence diversity within a read length increases. We demonstrate these findings on two control data sets, showing that SNV detection is more reliable on a high diversity human immunodeficiency virus sample as compared to a moderate diversity sample of hepatitis C virus. Finally, we show that in situations where probabilistic clustering retains false positive SNVs (for instance due to insufficient sample diversity or systematic errors), applying a strand bias test based on a beta-binomial model of forward read distribution can improve precision, with negligible cost to true positive recall.Conclusions: By combining probabilistic clustering (implemented in the program ShoRAH) with a statistical test of strand bias, SNVs may be called from deeply sequenced viral populations with high accuracy. \u00a9 2013 McElroy et al.; licensee BioMed Central Ltd.","authors":[{"name":"Beerenwinkel N."},{"name":"Bull R."},{"name":"Luciani F."},{"name":"McElroy K."},{"name":"Zagordi O."}],"citationCount":36,"date":"2013-07-24T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Genomics","title":"Accurate single nucleotide variant detection in viral populations by combining probabilistic clustering with a statistical test of strand bias"},"pmcid":"PMC3848937","pmid":"23879730","type":["Method"]},{"doi":"10.1089/cmb.2009.0164","metadata":{"abstract":"We present a computational method for analyzing deep sequencing data obtained from a genetically diverse sample. The set of reads obtained from a deep sequencing experiment represents a statistical sample of the underlying population. We develop a generative probabilistic model for assigning observed reads to unobserved haplotypes in the presence of sequencing errors. This clustering problem is solved in a Bayesian fashion using the Dirichlet process mixture to define a prior distribution on the unknown number of haplotypes in the mixture. We devise a Gibbs sampler for sampling from the joint posterior distribution of haplotype sequences, assignment of reads to haplotypes, and error rate of the sequencing process, to obtain estimates of the local haplotype structure of the population. The method is evaluated on simulated data and on experimental deep sequencing data obtained from HIV samples. \u00a9 Copyright 2010, Mary Ann Liebert, Inc.","authors":[{"name":"Beerenwinkel N."},{"name":"Geyrhofer L."},{"name":"Roth V."},{"name":"Zagordi O."}],"citationCount":72,"date":"2010-03-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Journal of Computational Biology","title":"Deep sequencing of a genetically heterogeneous sample: Local haplotype reconstruction and read error correction"},"pmid":"20377454","type":["Method"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Genetics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3053"},{"term":"Metagenomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3174"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"ShoRAH","home":"https://bio.tools/shorah","license":"GPL-3.0","version":["1.1.3","1.99.0"],"summary":"Inference of a population from a set of short reads. The package contains programs that support mapping of reads to a reference genome, correcting sequencing errors by locally clustering reads in small windows of the alignment, reconstructing a minimal set of global haplotypes that explain the reads, and estimating the frequencies of the inferred haplotypes.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"galaxy":{"version":"@VERSION@","first_commit":"2018-11-27","conda_name":"shorah","conda_version":"1.99.2","summary":"Reconstruct haplotypes using ShoRAH in amplicon mode","edam_operations":["Haplotype mapping","Variant calling"],"edam_topics":["Metagenomics","Sequencing","Genetics"],"toolshed_categories":["Sequence Analysis"],"toolshed_id":"shorah_amplicon","users_5_years":0,"users_all_time":0,"usage_5_years":0,"usage_all_time":0,"bio_tools_summary":"Inference of a population from a set